    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lysimachus.jpg" alt="Lysimachus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lisymachus,_marble_-_Ephesus_Museum.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a> of the narrow coastal strip on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> had formed a league of twelve small cities since at least the fifth century BCE, with a common sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Homarios near Aigion. Under the Macedonian kings after Alexander this older league broke apart, its cities held by garrisons or tyrants. Around 280 BCE, while the Macedonian kingdoms were in turmoil after the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, four western Achaean cities, Dyme, Patrae, Tritaia and Pharai, renewed the league. Aigion joined about five years later after expelling its garrison, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bura">Bura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keryneia">Keryneia</a>, whose tyrant Iseas resigned. At first the league had two generals and a common secretary; from 255 BCE a single general (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a></em>) led it, the first being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margus-of-keryneia">Margus of Keryneia</a>. The decisive expansion beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> began in 251 BCE, when the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> overthrew the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicocles-of-sicyon">Nicocles of Sicyon</a> and brought his city, which was not Achaean, into the league. In 243 BCE Aratus captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> from its Macedonian garrison in a night assault, and Corinth, Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus joined. Megalopolis followed in 235 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> in 229 BCE, making the league the leading power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/argo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argo.jpg" alt="Argo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_Volanakis_Argo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Konstantinos Volanakis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The member cities were mostly modest agricultural communities producing grain, olives, wine and livestock, though the league came to include important commercial centres such as Corinth, with its two harbours on the Isthmus, Sicyon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. Federal membership brought practical benefits. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> stresses that the members used the same laws, weights, measures and coinage, and citizens of one city probably enjoyed rights such as owning property and marrying in the others. The federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, small triobols bearing the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the monogram of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a>, were struck by many member cities. Achaean society was dominated by landowning families, who supplied the generals, cavalry commanders and envoys; Polybius, the son of the federal general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycortas">Lycortas</a>, came from such a family in Megalopolis. Tensions between rich and poor were acute in the third century BCE, and the programme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, with its promise of debt cancellation and land redistribution, attracted support in several Achaean cities, which helps explain why the league's leaders preferred alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to defeat by Sparta. Religion bound the league together through the federal cult of Zeus Homarios near Aigion, where the federal assemblies traditionally met, while the member cities kept their own cults and festivals, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmian-games">Isthmian Games</a> at Corinth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon.jpg" alt="Aratus of Sicyon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratus_of_Sicyon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The league's army was a citizen militia drawn from the member cities, supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> paid from federal funds, with a cavalry force under a federal hipparch and a small fleet under a navarch. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> it was weak in pitched battle: he excelled at night attacks and surprises such as the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>, but the league suffered a series of defeats at the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lykaion">Mount Lykaion</a>, Ladokeia and the Hekatombaion near Dyme in 227–226 BCE, which forced Aratus to call in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson of Macedon</a>. The army was transformed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen of Megalopolis</a>, who as hipparch and then, from 208 BCE, as general retrained the cavalry, re-equipped the infantry as a Macedonian-style <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> with long pikes and larger shields, and fostered a martial pride among the citizens. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-207-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 207 BCE he defeated the Spartan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machanidas">Machanidas</a>, reportedly killing him with his own hand. He later fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabis">Nabis of Sparta</a>, and after Nabis was murdered in 192 BCE he brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> into the league. The army's final campaign, in 146 BCE, ended in disaster: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/critolaus-achaean-general">Critolaus</a> was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-scarpheia">Scarpheia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaeus">Diaeus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Lucius Mummius</a> on the Isthmus, after which Corinth was sacked.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Achaean League was a true federal state, admired in antiquity and since. Member cities kept their own laws, magistrates and courts, but federal citizenship, foreign policy, war and federal finances belonged to the league. Its chief officer was the general (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a></em>), elected annually, who commanded the army and presided over the assemblies; he could not hold office in consecutive years, so leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a>, general seventeen times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s count, served in alternate years. He was assisted by ten <em>damiourgoi</em>, a hipparch, a secretary and a federal council. Regular assemblies (<em>synodoi</em>) met several times a year, at first at Aigion; their exact composition is debated, but they involved the council and citizens over thirty, while extraordinary assemblies (<em>synkletoi</em>), open to all citizens and voting by city, were summoned to decide on war and alliances. Around 188 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> had the meetings rotated among the member cities. The league arbitrated disputes between members and sent and received embassies as a single state. Its weakness was that larger members, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and Messene, had joined unwillingly and repeatedly sought to secede, giving outsiders, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, an opening to intervene in federal affairs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At its height in 191 BCE the Achaean League united the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> under a single government for the first time, something neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> had achieved by force. It was the most successful of the Hellenistic federal states, alongside its rival the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, and it showed that Greek cities could pool <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> without losing local self-government. Its fame owes much to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, who served as federal cavalry commander before being deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a hostage in 167 BCE, and whose <em>Histories</em> praise the league's equality and freedom of speech as a model of union. Through Polybius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s lives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a>, the league became a reference point in modern political thought. In <em>The Federalist</em> No. 18, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a>, writing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, examined the Achaean League as an example of a federal republic and of the dangers that foreign intervention posed to loose unions. The Victorian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-augustus-freeman">Edward Augustus Freeman</a> made it the centrepiece of his <em>History of Federal Government</em>. Philopoemen, called by a Roman, according to Plutarch, ‘the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>’, became a symbol of Greek independence, and the league's coinage and inscriptions remain central evidence for the study of ancient federalism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii.jpg" alt="Cleomenes III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kings_Agis_and_Cleomenes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michael Burghers, late 17th century, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The league's decline came from its dependence on stronger powers. To defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> it surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 224 BCE and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Doson</a>'s Hellenic alliance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a>, who had built the league on resistance to Macedon, ended his life as Philip V's ally; he died in 213 BCE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> poisoned on Philip's orders. In 198 BCE the league changed sides and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against Philip, which brought it Corinth again and the support it needed to absorb <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, Messene and Elis. Roman friendship came with Roman supervision. Spartan resentment, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> inflamed by abolishing the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycurgus-of-sparta">Lycurgus</a> in 188 BCE, gave Rome repeated grounds to intervene, and Philopoemen himself was captured and forced to take poison during a Messenian revolt in 183 BCE. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> the pro-Roman politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicrates-of-leontion">Callicrates</a> denounced his rivals, and about 1,000 leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a> were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. When disputes over Sparta revived and Rome demanded in 147 BCE that Sparta, Corinth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and other cities be detached, the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/critolaus-achaean-general">Critolaus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diaeus">Diaeus</a> led the league into war. After their defeats in 146 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Mummius</a> sacked Corinth and the league was dissolved; a much smaller Achaean league later revived with only local and religious functions.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Achaean League, a federation of Peloponnesian cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a>, was the most successful federal state of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, uniting the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> in 191 BCE before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> crushed it in 146 BCE; through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> it became a model for modern federal thinkers, including the authors of The Federalist.</em></p>

